    title: "Cancer takes the largest share, and the share swings hard from year to year",
    plain: "Across a quarter of a century cancer has taken more of the annual budget than any other field, about 38 percent on average. But the yearly figure is volatile: 19 percent in 2025, when an infant vaccine outbid it, and 52 percent in 2026.",
    detail: "The Ministry of Health's own published account of its committee states that from 1998 to 2023 oncology, including haemato-oncology, comprised the largest number and proportion of approved technologies, a mean of 38 percent, ahead of cardiovascular at 10 percent, neurology at 7 percent and endocrinology at 6 percent. The State Comptroller puts the oncology drug budget at 37 percent of the average annual supplement across 2016 to 2021. Individual years diverge sharply. About half the 2019 budget went to cancer. In 2021 it was 8 percent, which the Israeli authors attribute to pandemic priorities and a large diabetes allocation. In 2025, 124 million shekels of 650 million, about 19 percent, went to oncology, beaten for the first time by infectious disease at about 160 million, of which an infant respiratory syncytial virus vaccine alone took 109 million. In 2026 oncology and haemato-oncology took 52 percent, about 340 million shekels, while infectious disease fell from 24 percent to 1 percent. The other pressure is price: the Ministry reports that the average yearly cost of an oncology drug approved by the committee rose, inflation-adjusted, from about 60,000 shekels per patient in 2009 to over 130,000 in 2018, which it converts in the same sentence to about 18,000 and 43,000 US dollars. The oncology share for 2020, 2023 and 2024 could not be sourced and is not guessed here.",
    links: [IJTAHC, COMPTROLLER, ESMO_OPEN, { label: "Calcalist on the 2025 basket (6 February 2025, Hebrew, archived)", url: "https://web.archive.org/web/20260515133131/https://www.calcalist.co.il/local_news/article/syusfmfkke" }, YNET_2026],
