    plain: "The committee decides almost without health economics, publishes its transcripts months late, never tells rejected applicants why a Ministry forum screened them out, and does not check afterwards what the money bought. And about six in ten shekels go to ten technologies.",
    detail: "The State Comptroller's May 2022 report is the sharpest published critique and was read here in the original. Contrary to the 2010 procedure, which requires an economic evaluation to accompany an application, economic evaluations are almost never submitted: in the three years 2018 to 2020 the technology assessment division received one, and told the auditors it is not functionally equipped to carry out economic evaluations, in staff, tools or methods. The Ministry's technology forum, all of whose members are Ministry staff, rejected 46 percent of non-drug and 9 percent of drug requests over 2018 to 2021 without those rejections or their reasons reaching the committee or the applicant. The sub-committee that prices submissions has no public representative, admits no journalists where the main committee does, and publishes no protocols; the main committee's protocols were published 109 to 160 working days late over 2018 to 2020. Costings rest on disease registries with no power to compel reporting, and the Comptroller singles out the National Cancer Registry for holding little information on disease stage, which matters because cancer drugs were up to 37 percent of the addition over 2016 to 2021. Ten technologies took 62 percent of the whole addition over those years, 1.7 billion of about 2.7 billion shekels, with the remaining 40 percent spread across about a hundred; 31 percent of technologies added in 2016 to 2021 came at no cost. Despite that concentration the Ministry ran no specific monitoring of how the top technologies were used or what benefit they produced. Israeli oncologists are not uniformly comfortable either: in a survey of 127 of them, 59 percent said they often face dilemmas about recommending unfunded treatments to patients in financial difficulty without private insurance, and 38 percent feel uncomfortable discussing cost with patients. The Ministry's defence is that the model is participatory, transparent, fast to implement and publicly accepted, and one of the longest-running national health technology assessment systems in the world.",
    links: [COMPTROLLER, IJTAHC, { label: "Recommending unfunded innovative cancer therapies: ethical versus clinical perspectives among oncologists (Curr Oncol 2021)", url: "https://doi.org/10.3390/curroncol28040254" }, { label: "Shmueli, Do the equity-efficiency preferences of the Israeli Basket Committee match those of Israeli health policy makers? (Isr J Health Policy Res 2017)", url: "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13584-017-0145-4" }],
