    detail: "Section 47A of the Pharmacists Ordinance requires the Director to maintain a register of registered preparations and forbids anyone to manufacture, market or direct the use of a preparation unless it is registered and used in accordance with that registration. The Pharmacists Regulations (Preparations) of 1986 set the procedure. Two arms of the Ministry work in parallel on each dossier: the Drug Registration Department on safety and efficacy, and the Institute for Standardization and Control of Pharmaceuticals on quality. Clinical review goes to the Advisory Committee for Drug Registration, which takes at least two external expert opinions and can accept, accept with modifications, accept with post-approval requirements, reject, or hold for more data. The public drug registry is at israeldrugs.health.gov.il, with the Ministry's own caveat that some of its data may be incomplete or inaccurate. For scale, the advisory committee reviewed 620 new-drug and supplemental-indication applications between 2014 and 2019; in the 2014 to 2016 cohort oncology was the largest single field at 31 percent of applications, and oncology applications carried the highest risk of a major label variation after approval.",
