    detail: "The recognised country list sits in regulation 1 of the Pharmacists Regulations: the United States, Canada, any European Union member state, Switzerland, Norway, Iceland, Australia, New Zealand, Japan, Israel and, added by amendment in April 2022, Britain. A narrower list, without Canada, Britain or Israel, governs clinical trials and should not be confused with it. Three routes use the recognition. Section 47A(a2) of the Ordinance provides that where a preparation is identical to a registered one in active substance, form, strength, route, activity and bioavailability and has been approved for marketing by the FDA or the competent European authority, proof of that approval suffices, and the Director must register it within 70 days or give a reasoned refusal. For new drugs the Ministry runs a non-statutory abridged assessment relying on the pre-clinical evaluation already done abroad. And import approval under regulations 5A and 5B requires certificates from a recognised country. The Ministry's own published analysis is blunt about the limit: approval by the FDA or the EMA does not pave the way to automatic approval in Israel, and in a substantial proportion of cases the same clinical data produced different safety and efficacy conclusions, requiring more data or a rejection. The general statutory deadline is 270 days from submission, with a stop-clock while the applicant completes the file. Those are statutory deadlines, not measured performance: no study of observed Israeli approval times, and no head-to-head duration comparison with the FDA or EMA, could be found.",
