    detail: "Registration sits under section 47A of the Pharmacists Ordinance; funding sits under sections 8 and 9 of the National Health Insurance Law. The Israeli authors of the ESMO Open account put it plainly: coverage is universal, but not every licensed medicine or indication is reimbursed, and to be provided free a licensed cancer drug must also pass the Ministry's health technology assessment. The separation is visible in the regulator's own paperwork, which sets different forms depending on whether the preparation a hospital pharmacy is importing appears in the basket order for the requested indication. The only quantification that could be found is a proxy: of 3,284 compassionate treatments between 2020 and 2024, 60 percent of the commonest technologies, 13 of 22, were later included in the basket, typically after more than two years of compassionate use. No study of registration-to-reimbursement lag for Israeli oncology drugs was found.",
