    detail: "The Public Health Regulations (Medical Experiments on Human Subjects) of 1980 forbid a medical trial on a human in a hospital without written approval, subject to the Declaration of Helsinki, which is annexed to them. Approval requires the hospital Helsinki committee to have notified its approval in writing. Who the approving Director is depends on the trial: for a special medical trial as defined in the Fourth Schedule it is the hospital's own medical director, and for any other trial it is the Ministry's Director-General. Regulation 3B requires the opinion of the Supreme Helsinki Committee, a ten-member national body including two public members and six professors, for any trial touching a person's genetic makeup, any trial involving non-natural fertilisation, and anything else the Director refers. The Ministry's Procedure 14, in force since May 2020, classifies a complete application as special or not within two working days, requires material 15 working days before a meeting and 30 where a quality dossier is assessed, and sets decisions at 60 working days through the central and Supreme Committee route, 45 through the expert-opinion route and 30 for professional handling within the clinical trials department, excluding time waiting for the applicant. Sponsors must carry at least three million US dollars of liability insurance. Registration on the Ministry's own trials register, MyTrial, is a precondition of institutional approval and must be updated quarterly; registration on ClinicalTrials.gov is explicitly not.",
