    detail: "Talia Golan of Sheba Medical Center led POLO, a randomised placebo-controlled phase 3 of maintenance olaparib in metastatic pancreatic cancer with a germline BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation whose disease had not progressed on first-line platinum. Median progression-free survival was 7.4 months against 3.8, hazard ratio 0.53. The final overall survival analysis found no significant difference in medians, 19.0 against 19.2 months, but the curves separated late, with estimated three-year survival of 33.9 percent against 17.8 percent. The screening ratio, 3,315 screened for 154 randomised, is the practical case for population-level knowledge of carrier status rather than testing at the point of need. Downstream of that, Israel runs pancreatic surveillance in BRCA carriers and has published outcomes: in a multicentre cohort of 180 asymptomatic carriers followed a median four years, four adenocarcinomas and four grade 1 neuroendocrine tumours were found, all in BRCA2 carriers, two of them with no family history of pancreatic cancer, and three of the four cancers were resectable; a second Israeli prospective cohort of 239 high-risk people found pancreatic cancer in 11, 73 percent at an early stage. Breast surveillance changes stage too: in a Sheba cohort of 298 carriers, 19.8 percent of those who knew their status and were under surveillance presented with carcinoma in situ, against 5 percent of those who learned their status only after diagnosis.",
