    detail: "Written into the 2021 report, in the registry's own words: the Global Cancer Observatory figures are estimated, not measured, and the data for Israel there do not separate Arabs from Jews and others, and therefore the rates on that site cannot be compared with the true rates the registry measures. The structural consequence is that there is no official Israeli age-standardised all-cancer rate at all, only four. Stage has been recorded since 2000 but only insofar as the information reaches the registry, and cannot be assigned when there was no surgery, when treatment preceded the first pathology, or when the report came from a death notification; published completeness runs from about 86 percent for cervix and 79 percent for melanoma to about 72 percent for breast and 56 to 77 percent for colorectum. The State Comptroller's finding that the registry holds little information on stage is the same fact seen from the basket committee's side, where it makes the costing of cancer technologies unreliable. The registry's reportable set is tumour registration, not treatment: it records tumour characteristics, and since 2016 oestrogen, progesterone and HER2 status for invasive breast cancer, complete for 67 percent of 2018 diagnoses, but no evidence was found that it records treatment given. It does record vital status, which is what lets it publish relative survival, and it publishes that survival separately by sex and population group, which is a strength rather than a gap. The most recent general incidence and mortality update covers diagnosis year 2021 and was published in August 2024, a lag of about two years and eight months, and the registry warns that a year's first publication is provisional: its 2020 report reflected only about 92 percent of expected cases. Basal and squamous cell skin carcinomas are not reportable at all.",
